// # Fleettick Naming-Drift Check (Spec)
//
// Logline: Specify a read-only fleettick check that reports drift in
//          the fleet's rolling "latest" wip pointers — the check wC
//          proposed for the backlog in docs/latest-branch-naming.oot.
//
// Setup:   Accepted canonical form is wip/<host>-latest (manager,
//          2026-11-06). Evidence base: 233 wip refs across 97 repos
//          in the 2026-11-06 wip audit. This spec describes DETECTION
//          AND REPORTING ONLY — no deletes, no repoints, no merges.
//
// Beats:
//   1. What the check detects.
//   2. How it classifies each ref family.
//   3. What it emits into the tick report.
//   4. The gated migration path it feeds — never executes.

## 1. What the check detects

Per tick, for every swept repo, enumerate refs/heads/wip/* on the
canonical remote (github ls-remote — the fleet's GitHub-only policy
applies) and classify against four buckets:

  CANONICAL   wip/<host>-latest            — the accepted pointer
  LEGACY      wip/head-latest              — retired role-named form
              wip/spotlight-latest         — retired host-named form
  BUG         wip/<host>-<host>-latest     — doubled-token template scar
  SNAPSHOT    wip/<host>-snapshot-<ts>     — dated checkpoint family
                                             (separate, valid, skipped)

A repo is DRIFTED when any of these hold:

  a) MISSING-CANONICAL — repo carries >=1 legacy/bug latest ref but
     no wip/<host>-latest.
  b) MULTI-LATEST      — repo carries >=2 distinct latest-pointer
     refs (canonical or otherwise).
  c) TIP-CONFLICT      — the latest pointers it carries resolve to
     >=2 different commit tips. The audit proved these are parallel
     generations, not aliases: all 22 triple-carriers held 3 distinct
     tips.
  d) DOUBLE-TOKEN      — any ref matches wip/<x>-<x>-latest (same
     token twice). This is the writers' template bug
     (wip/<ws>-$(hostname)-latest where ws==hostname), not a naming
     choice.

## 2. What the check does NOT flag

  - Dated snapshots wip/<host>-snapshot-<yyyymmdd-hhmm>: checkpoints,
    not pointers; different lifecycle; explicitly out of scope.
  - Pane wips wip/<ws>-p<n>: active session branches, judged by the
    merged-or-not sweep logic, not by this check.
  - wip/autosnap-<host>: machine-state family, already canonical in
    its own namespace.
  - Refs present only on stale mirrors (origin/Forgejo): the check
    reads github only; mirror divergence is a separate report row.

## 3. Tick report emission

One line per repo per finding, in the reconcile section's style:

  NAMING <repo> MISSING-CANONICAL legacy=head-latest,spotlight-latest
  NAMING <repo> TIP-CONFLICT refs=3 tips=a1b2c3,d4e5f6,789abc
  NAMING <repo> DOUBLE-TOKEN ref=spotlight-spotlight-latest

Plus a rollup line in the summary section:

  naming=<repos-with-findings> missing=<n> conflict=<n> double=<n>

Severity mapping (matches the fleetdash v2 sev ladder):
  DOUBLE-TOKEN, TIP-CONFLICT  -> sev 2 (attention: split streams)
  MISSING-CANONICAL           -> sev 1 (drift: convention not adopted)
  MULTI-LATEST alone          -> sev 1
  clean single canonical      -> sev 0

The check is READ-ONLY: ls-remote + ref classification. It mutates
nothing, spools no alerts of its own (findings ride the tick report),
and fails closed: an unreachable remote yields NAMING <repo> REMOTE-ERR
at sev 3 rather than a skipped repo.

## 4. The gated migration it feeds (never executed by the check)

The report exists to size and order a human-gated migration:

  1. FIX WRITERS — correct the doubled-token template in whichever
     tool emits wip/<host>-<host>-latest; repoint all latest-writers
     to wip/<host>-latest.
  2. VERIFY CONSUMERS — grep fleet tooling for hardcoded
     head-latest / spotlight-latest reads before anything moves.
  3. REPOINT — create/move wip/<host>-latest per repo, choosing the
     tip by the TIP-CONFLICT report (the three streams are NOT
     interchangeable — each picks its own newest generation).
  4. HOLD — legacy and doubled refs stay in place, exactly like the
     31 merged pane wips: manager declined deletion.
  5. REMOVE — legacy refs only after explicit manager approval.

The check's job is to make step 3's tip-selection auditable and to
keep a running count of how much of the fleet still drifts after
writers are fixed — a burndown metric, not a mutator.
